WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.340 --> 00:00:04.530
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.530 --> 00:00:09.680
This is a neural network-based technique that
can perform audio-visual separation.

00:00:09.680 --> 00:00:13.660
Before we talk about what that is, I will
tell you what it is not.

00:00:13.660 --> 00:00:18.110
It is not what we've seen in the previous
episode where we could select a pixel and

00:00:18.110 --> 00:00:35.600
listen to it, have 

00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:42.890
a look.

00:00:42.890 --> 00:00:44.579
This one is different.

00:00:44.579 --> 00:00:50.370
This new technique can clean up an audio signal
by suppressing the noise in a busy bar, even

00:00:50.370 --> 00:00:53.399
if the source of the noise is not seen in
the video.

00:00:53.399 --> 00:01:03.900
It can also enhance the voice of the speaker
at the same time.

00:01:03.900 --> 00:01:17.670
Let's listen.

00:01:17.670 --> 00:01:29.669
Or, if we have a skype-meeting with someone
in a lab or a busy office where multiple people

00:01:29.669 --> 00:01:34.669
are speaking nearby, we can also perform a
similar speech separation, which would be

00:01:34.669 --> 00:02:11.660
a godsend for future meetings.

00:02:11.660 --> 00:02:30.780
And I think if you are a parent, the utility
this example needs no further explanation.

00:02:30.780 --> 00:02:38.290
I am not sure if I ever encountered the term
"screaming children" in the abstract of an

00:02:38.290 --> 00:02:41.219
AI paper, so that one was also a first here.

00:02:41.219 --> 00:02:47.640
This is a super difficult task, because the
AI needs to understand what lip motions correspond

00:02:47.640 --> 00:02:53.151
to what kind of sounds, which is different
for all kinds of languages, age groups, and

00:02:53.151 --> 00:02:54.610
head positions.

00:02:54.610 --> 00:03:01.749
To this end, the authors put together a stupendously
large dataset with almost 300.000 videos with

00:03:01.749 --> 00:03:03.540
clean speech signals.

00:03:03.540 --> 00:03:09.299
This dataset is then run through a multi-stream
neural network that detects the number of

00:03:09.299 --> 00:03:14.969
human faces within the video, generates small
thumbnails of them, and observes how they

00:03:14.969 --> 00:03:16.310
move over time.

00:03:16.310 --> 00:03:22.900
It also analyzes the audio signal separately,
then fuses these elements together with a

00:03:22.900 --> 00:03:27.439
recurrent neural network to output the separated
audio waveforms.

00:03:27.439 --> 00:03:32.329
A key advantage of this architecture and training
method is that as opposed to many previous

00:03:32.329 --> 00:03:38.430
works, this is speaker-independent, therefore
we don't need specific training data from

00:03:38.430 --> 00:03:40.800
the speaker we want to use this on.

00:03:40.800 --> 00:03:44.120
This is a huge leap in terms of usability.

00:03:44.120 --> 00:03:49.389
The paper also contains an excellent demonstration
of this concept by taking a piece of footage

00:03:49.389 --> 00:03:54.909
from Conan O'Brien's show where two comedians
were booked for the same time slot and talk

00:03:54.909 --> 00:03:56.279
over each other.

00:03:56.279 --> 00:04:01.260
The result is a performance where it is near
impossible to understand what they are saying,

00:04:01.260 --> 00:04:06.239
but with this technique, we can hear both
of them one by one, crystal clear.

00:04:06.239 --> 00:04:10.230
You see some results over here, but make sure
to click the paper link in the description

00:04:10.230 --> 00:04:12.300
to hear the sound samples as well.

00:04:12.300 --> 00:04:16.030
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

